INT. TAXIDERMY WORKSHOP - NIGHT

A cathedral of dead things. Foxes mid-leap, owls on bent branches, a bear reared upright with one paw raised as though about to speak. Glass eyes catch the light of a single green desk lamp.

INSPECTOR AMADI NWOSU (50s, long coat dripping, notebook in hand) stands in the doorway, letting his eyes adjust. At the back of the room, among tables strewn with scalpels and cotton wadding, a woman in a leather apron stands very still: MAGDALENA CROSS (40s, steady, sleeves rolled to the elbow, a smear of preservative on her cheek).

AMADI
Ms. Cross. You work late.

MAGDALENA
So do you.

She does not move. He walks in slowly, studying the shelves. Amadi stops at the bear and tilts his head.

AMADI
Beautiful piece. Whose is it?

MAGDALENA
A collector's.

AMADI
A collector who disappeared on Tuesday.

MAGDALENA
Did he? I'd have charged him for the storage.

Amadi crosses to the desk. A mug, half-drunk tea, a ledger. He turns a page with one finger.

AMADI
Your ledger shows three deliveries that night. Your neighbor says only one truck.

MAGDALENA
My neighbor likes trucks.

Behind the desk, a door ajar. A faint sound, a scrape. Amadi's eyes flick to it. Magdalena's do not.

AMADI
Who's back there?

MAGDALENA
Nobody.

The door creaks wider. A boy of fifteen's silhouette slips through, pale, a hoodie pulled up. He lunges for the exit. Amadi sidesteps and grabs his sleeve; the boy twists, Magdalena drops a scalpel on the table with a clang, and for one long second all three of them struggle in a knot of limbs beside the bear. The boy's hood falls back. DANTE CROSS (15, her son, hollow-eyed) glares at Amadi.

DANTE
Let go of me.

AMADI
Easy, son.

MAGDALENA
Dante, stop.

Dante stops. Amadi releases him. The boy backs away until he bumps into the bear and presses against its fur.

AMADI
(quietly)
Ms. Cross, where is he?

MAGDALENA
(a long beat)
I told you. A collector's.

Amadi's phone buzzes in his pocket. He ignores it. It buzzes again.

AMADI
(to Dante)
How old are you?

DANTE
Fifteen.

AMADI
Fifteen. And tonight you got up and did your mother's work, didn't you? Because she asked.

DANTE
(whisper)
She didn't ask.

Magdalena closes her eyes.

AMADI
I need to answer this.

He takes out the phone. Answers.

AMADI (CONT'D)
(into phone)
Nwosu.

DISPATCH (V.O.)
(over phone, tinny)
Inspector, we found the body. Riverbank, north of the bridge. He's been there since Tuesday. Cause of death, blunt trauma.

Amadi lowers the phone slowly. He looks at Magdalena. At Dante. At the bear, whose glass eyes seem to look back.

AMADI
(into phone)
Hold there.

He hangs up. The workshop is silent.

AMADI (CONT'D)
(to Magdalena)
So he never got stuffed.

MAGDALENA
No.

AMADI
Then what's in the back room?

Dante makes a small sound. Magdalena turns and walks to the door. She opens it wide. Inside, on a steel table under a sheet, a long shape.

MAGDALENA
The dog.

Amadi stares.

MAGDALENA (CONT'D)
His dog. He asked me to keep it. That's all he ever asked.

She pulls back the sheet. A greyhound, perfectly preserved, its eyes bright. Amadi lets out a long breath. Dante covers his face.

AMADI
(to the dog)
Well. That's not on the ledger either.

He pockets the notebook. Behind him the bear lifts its paw as if to wave goodbye.
